.th HASP VIII 5/31/77
.sh NAME
hasp \*- PWB/UNIX IBM Remote Job Entry
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd /usr/hasp/haspinit
.s3
.br
.bd /usr/hasp/hasphalt
.sh DESCRIPTION
.it Hasp
is the communal name for a collection of programs and a file
organization that allow PWB/UNIX,
equipped with an appropriate driver
for the DQS11-B,
to communicate with IBM's Job Entry Subsystems
by mimicking an IBM 2770 remote station.
.s3
.it Hasp
is initiated by the command
.it haspinit
and is terminated gracefully by the command
.it hasphalt.
While active,
.it hasp
runs in background and
requires no human supervision.
It quietly transmits to the IBM system jobs that have been queued by the command
.it send\^\c
(I)
and messages that have been entered by the command
.it rjestat\^\c
(I).
It receives from the IBM system print and punch data sets and
message output.
It enters the data sets into the proper PWB/UNIX directory
and notifies the appropriate user of their arrival.
It scans the message output to maintain a record on
each of its jobs.
It also makes these messages available
for public inspection,
so that
.it rjestat\^\c
(I),
in particular,
may extract responses.
.s3
Unless otherwise specified, all files and commands described below
live in directory
.it /usr/hasp
(first exceptions:
.it send
and
.it rjestat\^\c
).
.s3
There are two sources of
data that is to be transmitted by
.it hasp
from PWB/UNIX
to an IBM System/370.
In both cases, the data is organized as files in
.it ebcdic\^\c
(V)
format.
The first is a single file
.it haspmesg
that
is reserved for message input.
It is written by the enquiry command
.it rjestat\^\c
(I)
and is assigned a priority for transmission.
The second source, containing the bulk of the
data, consists of jobs that have been
entered into the
.if n .it xmit*
.if t .it xmit\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'
queue by the program
.it haspqer.
On completion of processing,
.it send
invokes
.it haspqer.
As each file is queued, a subordinate
.if n .it info/logx*
.if t .it info/logx\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'
file is created
to save the name, numeric id, login directory, and tty letter
of the user who is doing the queueing.
Upon successful transmission of the data to the IBM system,
.it haspdisp
will move this information into the
.it jobsout
file and delete the
.if n .it info/logx*
.if t .it info/logx\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'
file.
.s3
Each time
.it haspinit
is invoked, the
.if n .it xmit*
.if t .it xmit\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'
queue is compacted,
along with the associated
.if n .it info/logx*
.if t .it info/logx\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'
files,
and its beginning and end are calculated.
A three-digit sequence number specifying the first free slot
at the end of the queue is written to file
.it haspstat.
This number is subsequently updated by
.it haspqer
each time that a new job is entered into the queue.
A pointer to the beginning of the queue is maintained by
.it haspmain.
It is periodically compared to the current end of the queue
to determine whether any jobs are waiting to be transmitted.
A null lock-file
.it hasplock
is created with mode zero to prevent simultaneous updating of
.it haspstat.
.s3
In anticipation of receiving output,
.it hasp
always maintains a vacant file
.if n .it tmp*
.if t .it tmp\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'
in its own directory.
Output from the IBM system is initially written into this file and is
classified as either a print data set, a punch data set,
or message output.
Print output is converted to an ASCII text file,
with standard tabs.
Form feeds are suppressed, but the last line of each page
is distinguished by the presence of an extraneous
trailing space.
Punch output is converted to EBCDIC format.
This classification and
both conversions
occur as
the output is received;
.if n .it tmp*
.if t .it tmp\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'
files are
moved or copied
into the appropriate user's directory and assigned the name
.if n .it prnt*
.if t .it prnt\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'
or
.if n .it pnch*,
.if t .it pnch\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v',
respectively,
or
placed into user directories under user-specified names,
or used as input to
programs to be automatically executed,
as specified by the user.
This process is driven by the \*`usr=...\*' specification.
.it Hasp
retains ownership of these files and permits read-only access
to them.
Files of message output are digested by
.it hasp
immediately and are not retained.
.s3
A record is maintained for each job that passes through
.it hasp.
Identifying information is extracted contextually
from files transmitted to and received from the IBM system.
From each file transmitted,
.it hasp
extracts the job name, the programmer's name, the user name, the destination directory
name, and the
message level.
This information is temporarily stored, in the order of submission of jobs, in file
.it jobsout.
It is retrieved, by job name and programmer's name,
when the IBM system acknowledges the job and assigns a number to it.
.s3
The
IBM system automatically returns
an acknowledgement message for each job it receives.
Other status messages are returned in response to enquiries
entered by users and in response to enquiries that
.it hasp
itself generates every ten minutes.
All messages received by
.it hasp
are appended to the
.it resp
file.
The
.it resp
file is automatically truncated when it reaches 32,000 bytes.
Each sequence of enquiries written to the message file
.it haspmesg
should be preceded by an identification card image
of the form \*`/*$UX<process id>\*'.
The
IBM system will echo back the first portion of this card image,
as this is an illegal command.
The appearance of process ids in the response stream
permits responses to be passed on to the proper users.
.it Hasp
enters process id zero on all enquiries it generates on its own behalf.
.s3
While it is active,
.it hasp
occupies at least the two process slots that are appropriated by
.it haspinit.
These slots are used to run
.it haspmain,
that supervises data transfers,
as well as
.it haspdisp,
that performs dispatching functions;
these two processes are connected by a pipe.
The function of
.it haspmain
is to cycle repetitively, looking for data to
transfer either to or from the IBM system.
When it finds some, it spawns a child process, either
.it haspxmit
or
.it hasprecv,
to effect the transfer.
It waits for its child to complete its task and then
passes an event notice to
.it haspdisp.
.it Haspmain
exits normally as soon as it detects the file
.it haspstop
(created by
.it hasphalt\^\c
),
and exits reluctantly whenever it encounters a run of errors.
An attempt is made to manage the null file
.it haspdead
so that it exists precisely when
.it haspmain
is not executing.
.it Haspinit
has the capability of \fIdialing\fP any remote IBM system
with the proper hardware and software configuration.
A file
.it haspsoff
is created by
.it hasphalt
to signal that the phone should be hung up by
.it haspmain.
.s3
Ordinarily,
.it haspdisp
waits for event completion notices from
.it haspmain.
.it Haspdisp
follows up the events described by directing output files, updating
records, and notifying users.
It may spawn the program
.it haspcopy
to copy output across file systems.
.it Haspdisp
references the system files
.it /etc/passwd
and
.it /etc/utmp
to correlate user names, numeric ids, and terminals.
Normal termination of
.it haspmain
causes
.it haspdisp
to exit also.
In the case of error termination,
.it haspdisp
delays about one minute and then reboots
RJE
by executing
.it haspinit
again.
.s3
Event notices begin with a one-digit code.
The code \*`0\*' alone signals normal termination.
Other event notices consist of a code in the
range \*`1\*' to \*`6,\*' followed by the name of a file in
the
.it /usr/hasp
directory.
Notices are issued
as each file in the
.if n .it xmit*
.if t .it xmit\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'
queue is transmitted and as each
.if n .it tmp*
.if t .it tmp\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'
file is filled with output.
These files are moved to new temporary names before the event notice is composed.
Transmitted files (code 1) are renamed
.if n .it zmit*
.if t .it zmit\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'
and output files (codes 3-5) are renamed
.if n .it "prt*, pch*,"
.if t .it "prt\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v', pch\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v',"
or
.if n .it msg*,
.if t .it msg\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v',
depending on their type.
When
.it haspdisp
gets around to following up on the events described,
the files will either be deleted
or moved to a permanent destination.
.s3
Event notices are written to the
.it log
file at the time they are received by
.it haspdisp.
A typical section of the log looks as follows:
.s3
.nf
		1zmit283
		5msg61
		1zmit284
		5msg62
		3prt63
.fi
.s3
Additional lines are written to the log by
.it haspinit.
Each reboot of \fIhaspinit\fP is marked by a time stamp.
If the previous execution of
.it haspmain
ended in error, an exception notice
precedes the time stamp.
Exception notices are formatted by
.it haspmain
and consist of a sequence of capital letters.
The most common is \*`AAAAA\*', that indicates
five successive failures to acquire the line
for a transmission to the host.
A sequence of time stamps alternating with \*`AAAAA\*'
indicates that the host is not responding to RJE.
Each time the RJE facility is booted via the
.it haspinit
program,
the
.it log
file is cleaned out.
A copy of its last contents is placed in a file named
.it slog.
.s3
Several RJE programs, including the
.it send\^\c
(I)
command, use the 
.it ustat\^\c
(II)
system call to determine the remaining capacity
of the file systems they use,
in an attempt to keep roughly
1,000 blocks and 50 i-nodes free.
.it Haspinit
issues a warning when fewer than 2,000 blocks or 100 i-nodes are available.
.it Send
shuts down when only 1,500 blocks remain,
and
.it haspmain
stops accepting output from the IBM system
when the capacity falls to 1,200 blocks.
In addition, output files are limited in size
at all times to a maximum of 512K bytes.
Of this, only 64K bytes are guaranteed.
How much more output will be accepted
depends on the current capacity of the
.it hasp
file system.
Excess data is simply discarded,
with no provision for retrieving it.
.s3
Most
.it hasp
files and directories are protected
from unauthorized tampering.
The exception is the
.it pool
directory, that is provided so that
.it send\^\c
(I)
can create temporary files in the correct file system.
.it Haspqer
and
.it rjestat\^\c
(I),
the user's interfaces to
.it hasp,
operate in \fIsetuid\fP mode to contribute the necessary permission modes.
.it Rjestat\^\c
(I),
incidentally, extends to anyone who can login as
.it rje
complete freedom to enter console commands.
When invoked with a \fB+\fP argument,
it suppresses the \fBd\fP that begins a display command
and allows one to cancel or re-route jobs.
.s3
Some minimal oversight of each
.it hasp
subsystem is required.
The
.it hasp
mailbox should be inspected and cleaned out periodically.
The
.it job
directory should also be checked.
The only files placed there are output files whose
destination file systems are out of space.
Users should be given a short period of time (say, a day or two),
and then these files should be removed.
In the source code for the program,
.it haspdisp
is a \fIdefine\fP statement for the character string \s-1\fBSFILE\fP\s0.
If it is set to 1,
all returned jobs for which PWB/UNIX RJE can not
find the \*`usr=\fB...\fP\*' specification are placed
into the
.it job
directory.
This feature is primarily intended for those PWB/UNIX systems that
are connected to the IBM systems whose output format
is not known to
.it haspdisp.
.s3
Usage statistics are recorded in the directory
.it /usr/hasp/usg,
if it exists.
Six files will be created and updated.
Each will contain data on a per-user-id basis.
File
.it hasp.in.sum
accumulates
the number of blocks transmitted by
.it hasp;
file
.it hasp.in.cnt
records the number of transmissions; file
.it hasp.in.max
records the size, in blocks,
of the largest job sent.
Files
.it "hasp.out.sum, hasp.out.cnt"
and
.it hasp.out.max
contain the same statistics
for output received by
.it hasp.
The program
.it usage
may be used to print these statistics;
\*`usage file [user-id1 ...]\*'
will print out the statistics gathered in \fIfile\fP.
If the optional user-id list is present, only the statistics for these user-ids will be printed.
.s3
The configuration table
.it /usr/rje/lines
is accessed by all components of RJE.
Its six columns may be labeled
\*`host\*', \*`system\*', \*`directory\*', \*`prefix\*', \*`device\*',
and \*`types\*'.
Each line of the table (maximum of six)
defines an RJE connection.
\*`Host\*' is the name of a remote computer: \fBA, B,\fP
or \fB1110\fP.
\*`System\*' is a string of capital letters identifying PWB/UNIX systems.
The first specifies where the RJE connection is normally terminated;
the remainder specify where it may be backed-up to
if the primary RJE system goes down.
\*`Directory\*' is the directory name of the servicing RJE subsystem.
\*`Prefix\*' is the string prepended (redundantly) to
several crucial files and programs in the directory: \fIhasp, hasp2, uvac\fP.
\*`Device\*' is the name of the controlling \%DQS-11B,
with \*`\fI/dev/\fP\*' excised.
\*`Types\*' contains information on the type of connection to make.
It contains the logon id and
phone number to use when RJE is to automatically \fIdial\fP a remote IBM system.
An \fBn\fP in this field indicates that this
entry is not for dial-up.
When a dial-up entry is initiated,
the phone number found here is automatically dialed,
PWB/UNIX RJE logs on,
and normal RJE processing
occurs.
When
.it hasphalt
is executed,
RJE signs off and hangs up the phone automatically.
If the first character in the \*`types\*' field is an \fBi\fP, all console status
facilities are inhibited
(e.g.,
.it rjestat\^\c
(I)
will not behave like a status terminal, and
the ten-minutes automatic status inquiry is inhibited).
.s3
The file
.it /usr/rje/sys
contains the single-letter name of the current PWB/UNIX system.
An RJE connection will be considered available
if this is its primary system or if this is one of its backup
systems and the associated directory is mounted.
.it Send\^\c
(I)
and
.it rjestat\^\c
(I)
select an available connection by indexing on the \*`host\*' field
of the configuration table.
.it Hasp
programs index on the \*`prefix\*' field.
A subordinate directory,
.it sque,
exists in
.it /usr/rje
for use by
.it haspdisp
and
.it shqer
programs.
This directory holds those output files that
have been designated as standard input to some executable
file.
This designation is done via the \*`usr=...\*' specification.
.it Haspdisp
places the output files here and updates the file
.it log
to specify the order of execution, arguments to be passed, etc.
.it Shqer
executes the appropriate files.
A program
called
.it compact
compacts the
.it log
file.
It should be executed before
.it shqer
and RJE have been started.
.s3
All HASP programs are reentrant; therefore,
if more than one HASP is to be run on a given
PWB/UNIX system,
simply link,
via
.it ln\^\c
(I),
HASP2
program names to HASP names in
.it /usr.
.ne 10
.sh FILES
configuration-dependent and general-purpose RJE files:
.br
.s3
/dev/rjei		DQS11-B
.br
/dev/tty?		terminals
.br
/etc/utmp		list of active users
.br
/etc/passwd		user population
.br
/usr/rje/sys		PWB/UNIX system name, e.g., \*`A\*'
.br
/usr/rje/lines		PWB/UNIX RJE lines configuration table
.br
/usr/rje/sque/log	log information for \fIshqer\fP
.s3
user files
.s3
.br
.if n */.mail			a user's mailbox
.if t \v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'/.mail			a user's mailbox
.br
.if n */prnt*			a user's print data set
.if t \v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'/prnt\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'			a user's print data set
.br
.if n */pnch*			a user's punch data set
.if t \v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'/pnch\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'			a user's punch data set
.s3
hasp files (relative to the
.it directory
entry in the RJE configuration table):
.s3
.br
.if n hasp*			mostly programs
.if t hasp\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'			mostly programs
.br
haspdead		inactive flag
.br
haspsoff		dial-up hang-up signal
.br
haspstop		halt signal
.br
haspmesg		message slot
.br
haspstat  		queue end record
.br
hasplock		lockout file
.br
.if n xmit*			jobs queued
.if t xmit\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'			jobs queued
.br
.if n info/logx*		haspqer loginfo
.if t info/logx\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'		haspqer loginfo
.br
.if n job/*			output from jobs whose file systems are out of space
.if t job/\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'			output from jobs whose file systems are out of space
.br
jobsout			fifo job store
.br
.if n tmp*			output files
.if t tmp\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'			output files
.br
log			event log
.br
resp			concatenated responses from the IBM system
.br
status			RJE message of the day
.br
.if n pool/stm*		\fIsend\fP\^(I) temporaries
.if t pool/stm\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'		\fIsend\fP\^(I) temporaries
.br
.if n usg/*			usage statistics
.if t usg/\v'.2v'*\v'-.2v'			usage statistics
.sh "SEE ALSO"
send(I), rjestat(I), rje(IV), ebcdic(V), regen(VIII)
.br
.it "Guide to IBM Remote Job Entry for PWB/UNIX Users"
by A. L. Sabsevitz.
.br
.it "System Components: IBM 2770 Data Communication System,"
IBM SRL \%GA27-3013.
.br
.it "OS/VS2 HASP II Version 4 System Programmer's Guide"
IBM SRL \%GC27-6992.
.sh DIAGNOSTICS
.it Haspinit
provides brief error messages describing obstacles to
bringing up
.it hasp.
They can best be understood in the context of the RJE source code.
The most frequently occurring one is \*`cannot open /dev/rjei\*'.
This may occur if the \%DQS-11B status register shows something other than
READY (octal 200).
It will also occur if another process already has the \%DQS-11B open,
or if the exclusive use flag (_dqsx+3, _dqsx+73, etc.)
has remained set after a close of the \%DQS-11B.
.s3
Once
.it hasp
has been started, users should assist in monitoring its performance,
and should notify operations personnel
of any perceived need for remedial action.
.it Rjestat\^\c
(I)
will aid in diagnosing the current state of RJE.
It can detect, with some reliability,
when the far end of the communications line has gone dead,
and will report in this case that the host computer
is not responding to RJE.
It will also attempt to reboot
.it hasp
if it detects a prolonged period of inactivity on the \%DQS-11B.
.sh BUGS
The name
.it hasp
is an anachronism.
It is used only
as a collective name and could represent
.it hasp,
.it jes2,
.it asp,
etc.
